Ladybird reports the events from RequestServer, which is the only
process that sees a request’s final outgoing headers (it adds the
Cookie header) and its response headers before Set-Cookie and HSTS
are stored. That’s the same place in the request’s life as Firefox’s
http-on-modify-request and http-on-examine-response observers in
WebRequest.sys.mjs, and Chromium’s WebRequestProxyingURLLoaderFactory,
which sits between the renderer and the network service. Both require
host access to the request URL and to the document that made it, as
Ladybird does: Firefox in ChannelWrapper::Matches, Chromium through
WebRequestPermissions::CanExtensionAccessURL. Neither lets an extension
see another extension’s requests.

The error names in onErrorOccurred are Firefox’s NS_ERROR_*
names for the matching conditions (NS_ERROR_UNKNOWN_HOST,
NS_ERROR_CONNECTION_REFUSED, NS_BINDING_ABORTED for a request the page
stopped waiting for), since Firefox builds of extensions compare them;
Chromium reports net::ERR_* names instead.
